WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.840
If you want to get your life together,

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:08.680
you have to start with small boring

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:11.320
habits to fix the loops that create 95%

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:13.520
of our problems. I was once a homeless

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:16.360
teenager, [music] trained as a monk,

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:18.520
went to MIT, became a CEO, a board

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:20.960
member, and none of that was about

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:21.640
dreaming harder. So, forget motivation.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.520
[music]

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:26.200
In this video, I'll walk you through

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.360
nine micro habits backed by science

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.600
>> [music]

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:30.880
>> that protect the three most important

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:33.760
things your life runs on. Your

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:36.360
attention, your energy, and your

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:39.840
happiness. Let's get into it. You have

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:41.880
big, ambitious, exciting goals, so why

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.320
are we talking about small boring little

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:46.920
habits? A very dear friend of mine has

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:49.280
climbed Mount Everest twice. Now, I

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:51.720
haven't seen him lately, so knowing him,

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:54.760
it may be three times by now. And I

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:57.640
asked him what he remembered from his

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:59.440
experience of the actual climb, and he

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:01.400
simply said, "You know, when you're

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.840
climbing Mount Everest,

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:07.240
don't look up. If you look up, the

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:11.480
mountain can break you. Because above

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:13.840
8,000 m, you enter what climbers call

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:16.120
the death zone, where the oxygen

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:18.920
pressure is too low for the body to

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:21.960
survive. So, every step becomes

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:24.400
expensive. Your lungs are fighting, your

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:26.720
legs are burning, your brain does not

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.280
have enough oxygen, so your judgment

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:31.920
gets weaker." And his advice was once

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.080
again brutally simple. "Keep your eyes

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.800
down.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:39.440
Take a step.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:41.800
Then pause. Gather yourself. Then decide

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:44.240
to take the next step. And what he was

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:47.000
saying has a lot to do with how big

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:49.200
goals feel to us and our brains. [music]

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:51.760
You want to train for a marathon, you

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:55.080
want to write a book, build a business,

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:58.680
fix your finances. From far away, all

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:01.040
these goals look daunting. And it's like

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:03.840
climbing Mount [music] Everest. But what

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:07.479
we have to do is to break those goals

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:09.200
down into specific steps. And then take

00:02:07.479 --> 00:02:12.080
the first [music] step.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:13.840
And then pause and take the second step.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:15.800
This is where habits become so

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.520
incredibly important because they can

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:20.880
stop our brain from pretending that we

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:23.320
can climb the entire mountain all at

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.000
once. So the idea underneath [music]

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:29.280
all these habits is to turn your

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:32.120
ambition into a small set of actions.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.360
Now, you probably seen hundreds of habit

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:37.120
videos on YouTube. And they're all great

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:39.280
and they have great ideas. Like stack

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:41.080
your habits or put the phone away in the

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:44.000
next room when you're doing deep work.

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:46.760
These are very useful ideas. But this

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.959
video is not about focusing on those

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:51.240
habits that you already know. Cuz you've

00:02:48.959 --> 00:02:52.959
heard them many times before. We want to

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:55.680
focus on the fundamental [music]

00:02:52.959 --> 00:02:58.320
question. Which habits actually change

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:00.560
the architecture of your life. Let's

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:03.400
start with the three attention habits.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:05.400
Our first habit is correct [music] after

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:08.440
the click. You know when we're flying,

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:10.800
the pilot puts the airplane on autopilot

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:13.720
mode once they reach the cruising

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.192
altitude. And the plane is almost never

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:15.400
exactly on course.

00:03:15.192 --> 00:03:18.760
>> [music]

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:21.640
>> Wind pushes it or the path curves. And

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:24.720
the autopilot is constantly making these

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:26.400
small corrections. The plane doesn't fly

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:29.602
in a straight [music] line.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.200
It flies in a series of recoveries.

00:03:29.602 --> 00:03:31.680
>> [music]

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:34.400
>> But it still gets you where you need to

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:37.000
go, right? And our attention is very

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:39.427
similar to. Most of us try to catch the

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:41.640
bad habit before it happens, right?

00:03:39.427 --> 00:03:44.400
[music] You tell yourself, "I will not

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:46.959
pick up my phone during deep work." Or

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:50.280
"I will not eat that ice cream." Which

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:53.080
is my weakness, by the way. But then you

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:55.480
do it anyway. Because well, clicking on

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:57.640
that app has become a habit. Eating that

00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:01.440
ice cream has become [music] a habit. It

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:04.560
is now a reflex. But the new habit is

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:07.920
this, correct after the click. The

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:10.240
moment you notice the reflex has fired

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:14.142
after you have acted on it,

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:14.240
attach a good habit. One useful action

00:04:14.142 --> 00:04:16.280
>> [music]

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:18.239
>> immediately after. So, let's say you

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:20.440
clicked on the app. But then instead of

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:22.080
using that app, just get up and get a

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:24.280
glass of water or [music] do deep

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:26.640
breathing. That's the real window of

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:29.360
opportunity. In a way,

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:31.840
you're interrupting the interruption.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:34.440
Make sure your action is small and easy

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:36.320
to do because the last thing you need is

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:38.120
another mountain to climb when you've

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:40.040
already slipped. So, that's our first

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:42.000
habit. Let's move to the second

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:44.800
attention habit, controlling [music]

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.200
your input. A psychologist named Bluma

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:49.960
Zeigarnik noticed something very

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:52.788
strange. Waiters [music] in a cafe could

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:53.000
remember a lot of complex

00:04:52.788 --> 00:04:56.320
>> [music]

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:58.680
>> unpaid orders perfectly. But the moment

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:01.160
the bill was settled, the order [music]

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:04.200
vanished from their memory. She tested

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:06.120
it and found the same pattern in other

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:09.240
professions, too. If something [music]

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:11.880
is unfinished, your brain keeps reaching

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:14.040
for it. Every email you opened but

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:16.240
didn't reply to, every meeting that

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:18.360
ended without a clear next step, they

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:21.240
stay open in your brain [music] in the

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:23.080
background. Like an app on your phone

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:25.640
you forgot to close and is draining

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:28.240
resources. So, the habit of controlling

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:31.040
the input has two steps. First, [music]

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:34.480
do your process in batches. When I was a

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:38.000
CEO, I had two or three fixed windows a

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:40.560
day, [music] 9:00 a.m., 1:00 p.m., 4:30

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:43.040
maybe, and that's when I checked emails

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:46.800
and Slack. If something was truly

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:48.960
urgent, my team knew how to find me. But

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:52.080
outside of those batches,

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:53.120
I gave myself my time. And second, when

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:53.704
you're going through your batch of

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:53.880
items,

00:05:53.704 --> 00:05:57.920
>> [music]

00:05:53.880 --> 00:06:00.259
>> touch each item once, and only once. And

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:01.320
this is a little difficult, and I have

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:03.720
[music]

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:06.120
a decision framework that I call 4D

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:07.040
framework, and it has four options: do

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:10.880
it,

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:12.840
or delegate it, or date it, or delete

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:14.664
it. So, for example, let's say you open

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:15.040
an email. Now, you have four options:

00:06:14.664 --> 00:06:17.480
>> [music]

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:20.600
>> finish responding it, or forward it to

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:23.600
someone in your team, or snooze it,

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:25.320
or get rid of it. Otherwise, that thing

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:27.600
will linger on and on forever.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:29.240
Controlling these inputs gives your

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:31.560
brain [music] permission to put things

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:34.160
down, to finish it. And by the way, if

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:36.960
you like to read more about such ideas,

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:39.554
I write a short newsletter once a week.

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:39.640
One useful idea, one tool,

00:06:39.554 --> 00:06:41.960
>> [music]

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:44.880
>> and one practice. You can subscribe

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:46.120
below. I love writing them,

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:48.960
and it's free.

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:52.320
The third attention habit is perhaps the

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:55.400
most boring one: write to think. Jeff

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:57.960
Bezos built one of the strongest [music]

00:06:55.400 --> 00:07:00.560
and strangest meeting cultures in

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:03.080
corporate America. At Amazon, senior

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:05.120
meetings often did not start with

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:07.320
someone presenting a deck.

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:09.280
They began in silence, [music]

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:12.480
because the executives would sit in the

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:15.480
meeting room and read a written memo for

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:18.160
about 15 minutes. Why do that? Because

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:20.820
Bezos believed that PowerPoint can hide

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:21.000
sloppy thinking, but a written paragraph

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:23.400
>> [music]

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:27.520
>> has to connect one thought to the next

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:30.160
one. Writing a narrative creates more

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:32.160
boring [music] work, but it also creates

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:34.520
more clarity. And it doesn't matter what

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:37.680
you write, if it's journaling or

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:40.400
gratitude diary or a business proposal,

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:42.800
writing things down by hand is the best

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:44.480
way to clarify your thinking.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:47.760
And there is a difference between

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:50.760
writing and typing. Researchers from

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:54.080
Princeton and UCLA found that students

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:56.360
who wrote notes by hand performed better

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:58.200
on conceptual questions than students

00:07:56.360 --> 00:08:00.040
who typed [music] on laptops. Writing

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:02.640
isn't just about recording what you

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:04.440
think. It helps you find out what you're

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:07.560
thinking. [music] So, here's the small

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:10.680
habit. Before a hard conversation,

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:12.565
before a big decision, before anything

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:12.800
that feels foggy,

00:08:12.565 --> 00:08:15.240
>> [music]

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:16.680
>> try writing it down. Write three things.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:18.440
What do I know?

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:20.760
What am I assuming?

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:23.640
What is the next honest action? In most

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:25.010
cases, the fog will lift. You don't need

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:25.200
to fill the page.

00:08:25.010 --> 00:08:27.880
>> [music]

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:29.680
>> You just have to empty your mind.

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:32.520
So, those are the three attention

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:34.880
habits. Now, let's go to our first

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:37.560
energy [music] habit. It is the smallest

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:40.120
and the most dangerous one. Energy

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:43.400
doesn't always flow smoothly like a

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:46.440
continuous stream. In 1900, a physicist

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:49.000
named Max Planck discovered that energy

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:51.400
moves in these tiny [music] discrete

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:54.240
packets. He called that packet a

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:55.800
quantum. It was so radical an idea that

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:57.800
even [music] Planck himself didn't

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:59.726
believe the mathematics behind it. But,

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:00.280
that idea became one of the foundations

00:08:59.726 --> 00:09:02.600
[music]

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:05.320
of quantum mechanics. In fact, years

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:08.960
later, the world learned the terrifying

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:10.920
version of the same idea. In 1945, the

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:13.000
Manhattan Project did [music] something

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:14.791
that changed the world forever. They

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:17.320
took the smallest thing in the nature,

00:09:14.791 --> 00:09:20.400
[music] the atom, and split it. And the

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:23.040
energy released from that single act

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:25.400
destroyed two cities. Oppenheimer, the

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:28.680
man [music] who led that project,

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:29.800
watched the first test and quoted from

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:31.160
the Indian scripture, [music]

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:33.440
Bhagavad Gita,

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:35.520
>> Now I am become death,

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:38.240
the destroyer of worlds.

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:41.120
>> The smallest unit in the nature holds

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:44.080
the most power. That's why that is our

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:45.880
first energy habit, quantize the action.

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:48.400
Break your habit into small [music]

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:50.760
packets because the hardest part of any

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:53.120
habit is starting it. If you want to

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:54.840
write a book, writing the first line is

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:56.520
the hardest. If you want to clean up

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:58.240
your kitchen, picking up the first dish

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:00.360
is the hardest. So, here's the action

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:03.320
item. It's pretty simple. Quantize your

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:06.520
habit, writing one line, doing half a

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:09.200
push-up, walking for 1 minute. It's all

00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:11.280
about reducing the force required to

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:13.520
start. Now, let's go to the second

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:16.320
energy habit, something that even I

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:19.080
don't like doing, but it must be done.

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:21.520
There was a study done in 2013 that

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:23.567
tested the effect of caffeine at three

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:23.760
different times before bed.

00:10:23.567 --> 00:10:25.640
>> [music]

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:28.160
>> Right before sleep,

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:30.240
3 hours before, and 6 hours [music]

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:33.080
before. And they found that caffeine

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:35.560
that was taken even 6 hours before

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:38.120
significantly disrupted your sleep.

00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:41.160
Because caffeine has a half-life of

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:44.240
about 5 or 6 hours. That means if you

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:46.680
drink a cup of coffee at 4:00 p.m., half

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:48.920
of that caffeine is still circulating in

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:50.720
your system at 9:00 p.m. And who knows,

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:52.720
a quarter of it might still be there

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:54.800
after [music] midnight. So, you feel

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:56.480
like you slept well, but you're not

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:58.960
getting the deep [music] rest that you

00:10:56.480 --> 00:11:01.520
needed. And then you wake up tired and

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:03.480
groggy, and what's the solution? You

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:05.920
reach for another cup of coffee, and the

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:07.480
cycle feeds itself. So, one habit I

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:10.320
started [music] recently is to cut

00:11:07.480 --> 00:11:12.640
caffeine at least 6 hours before bedtime

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:16.040
because the most productive thing you

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:18.839
can do for your career, your clarity,

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:21.760
any relationships, is [music] to protect

00:11:18.839 --> 00:11:24.160
your sleep. I love coffee, but the

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:26.640
coffee that can save your afternoon can

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:29.040
also sabotage your next morning. So, the

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:31.240
small habit is to have [music] a clear

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:33.320
cut-off time for your caffeine. And the

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:36.240
third energy habit is about riding the

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:39.360
waves. Your afternoon crash is not a

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:41.560
failure. It's a signal. Your brain is

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:44.280
not [music] a machine. A scientist named

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:46.280
Nathaniel Kleitman was a father of

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:48.240
modern sleep research, [music] and he

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:51.480
discovered that your brain doesn't just

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:53.640
cycle at night. It moves in cycles even

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:56.840
during the daytime. Kleitman found that

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:59.853
most of us have an 80 [music] to 120

00:11:56.840 --> 00:12:00.120
minutes wave of high and low alertness.

00:11:59.853 --> 00:12:02.880
>> [music]

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:06.520
>> He called them ultradian cycles. Peak

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:09.080
focus, then a dip. Peak, then a dip. All

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:10.720
day long. That's how the brain is built.

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:13.120
Now, the problem is our modern work

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:16.000
habits refuse to accept that idea. Our

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:19.240
work days are still built to reflect the

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:22.040
factory era. Go to the office, produce 8

00:12:19.240 --> 00:12:23.840
to 10 hours of output non-stop, push

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:25.960
through the dip, [music] drink coffee,

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:28.280
push again, and then when our brain

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:31.280
collapses at 3:30 in the afternoon, we

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:32.240
think, "Oh my god, I lack focus. I lack

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:34.800
discipline." [music]

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:36.520
But, it's neither. It's just that our

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:39.200
brain works in waves. [music]

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:41.680
Every wave of effort needs a trough of

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:43.738
recovery. And here's the part most

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:45.400
people get wrong. They take a break,

00:12:43.738 --> 00:12:48.200
[music] but then they fill it with more

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:49.480
input and stimulation. A real break has

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:52.880
to [music] be

00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:55.120
a real break. Stand up and walk for 10

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:56.920
minutes. If you meditate, go ahead and

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:58.839
do that. If you want to [music] do deep

00:12:56.920 --> 00:13:01.040
breathing, go ahead and do that. So,

00:12:58.839 --> 00:13:02.733
here's the action item for the habit.

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:02.960
Pick a focus block,

00:13:02.733 --> 00:13:06.400
>> [music]

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:09.080
>> maybe 60 minutes, maybe 90. Then, pick a

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:12.920
real recovery block. [music] 10 minutes,

00:13:09.080 --> 00:13:13.920
20 minutes with no feed, no inbox, no

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:15.360
new input. [music]

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:18.520
Just recover.

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:19.764
Then, write the next wave. And now onto

00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:20.080
the happiness habits.

00:13:19.764 --> 00:13:22.480
>> [music]

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:24.920
>> You know, when life gets heavy, I have

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:26.160
this small habit that seems completely

00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:28.360
useless.

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:29.880
I go outside at night

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:32.160
and I look up.

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:35.280
That's my favorite tiny habit because

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:39.414
your story and mine [music] is the story

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:41.480
of the universe and it is a story 13.8

00:13:39.414 --> 00:13:43.840
[music] billion years in the making.

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:46.920
When you look up, you see billions of

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:49.040
galaxies each beaming with billions of

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:51.760
stars. Any [music] one of them could

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:54.640
hold planets like ours and here's the

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:58.160
strangest part. The sky that you're

00:13:54.640 --> 00:14:00.120
looking at is not a live telecast. Light

00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:02.520
takes time to travel.

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:04.600
Some of the stars you'll see tonight

00:14:02.520 --> 00:14:06.680
sent their light hundreds or even

00:14:04.600 --> 00:14:09.544
thousands [music] of years ago. Some of

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:11.240
them may have already died and we won't

00:14:09.544 --> 00:14:14.200
[music] know for centuries. We're

00:14:11.240 --> 00:14:17.560
looking at a memory from the past. The

00:14:14.200 --> 00:14:20.560
stars above us are ghosts. Now, whether

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:22.644
you believe in a religious explanation

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:22.800
or a scientific one,

00:14:22.644 --> 00:14:25.840
>> [music]

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:28.440
>> the mystery, the miracle,

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:31.800
and the magic of our existence in this

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:32.470
universe is undeniable. So, the first

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:32.680
habit

00:14:32.470 --> 00:14:35.160
>> [music]

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:37.280
>> is to look up. And then comes the second

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:41.400
move. Look at yourself in [music] the

00:14:37.280 --> 00:14:44.320
mirror and I mean really look. The same

00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:45.880
atoms that made those stars above made

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:48.880
you and me.

00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:52.040
We [music] are the same stardust.

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:55.360
There is no one like you on this planet.

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:59.280
Your DNA, your exact [music] memories,

00:14:55.360 --> 00:15:01.720
your scars, your voice, your childhood,

00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:04.280
your [music] private fears, there's only

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:06.920
one version of you. By the time I

00:15:04.280 --> 00:15:09.805
finished speaking [music] this sentence,

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:10.640
roughly 30 million cells in your body

00:15:09.805 --> 00:15:13.240
[music]

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:15.700
may have been replaced. I took roughly 8

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:18.320
seconds to say that. So, the math will

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:20.680
[music] work out, but it's fascinating

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:23.400
that you're changing every second and

00:15:20.680 --> 00:15:25.525
yet [music] you feel exactly the same.

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:25.840
Okay, so you're going to say, "Fine.

00:15:25.525 --> 00:15:28.520
>> [music]

00:15:25.840 --> 00:15:30.765
>> What does this have to do with small

00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:31.200
habits and happiness?" Well,

00:15:30.765 --> 00:15:33.160
>> [music]

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:36.320
>> we just covered two important happiness

00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:38.600
habits. Because when you look up, you're

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:41.760
reminded [music] that you're surrounded

00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:43.760
by millions of miracles. And when you

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:46.000
look [music] down, you're reminded that

00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:48.480
you're one of those miracles. Those two

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:52.240
habits [music] lead to awe and

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:54.080
gratitude. Which then leads to the ninth

00:15:52.240 --> 00:15:57.680
and the final habit. [music]

00:15:54.080 --> 00:16:00.200
The habit of forgiving yourself when all

00:15:57.680 --> 00:16:02.800
your habits break. Because they will

00:16:00.200 --> 00:16:05.520
break. Your [music] systems will break

00:16:02.800 --> 00:16:08.560
and your dreams will shatter.

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:10.640
All of that is inevitable. But don't get

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:13.680
trapped in any spiral [music]

00:16:10.640 --> 00:16:15.961
of shame. All of us have days and weeks

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:16.640
where nothing is going our way, right?

00:16:15.961 --> 00:16:17.600
[music]

00:16:16.640 --> 00:16:20.720
And

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:23.080
when every day is a firefight, none of

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:25.400
these small habits will survive. [music]

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:28.120
But that's okay.

00:16:25.400 --> 00:16:29.680
That is the point. You don't have to be

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:32.680
your best self [music] on your worst

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:35.839
day. That kind of self-prosecution

00:16:32.680 --> 00:16:36.921
is way more exhausting than missing a

00:16:35.839 --> 00:16:37.400
habit.

00:16:36.921 --> 00:16:40.640
>> [music]

00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:43.080
>> And that's why our last small habit

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:46.440
is self-forgiveness. The three small

00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:48.600
habits, awe, gratitude, [music] and

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:50.880
self-forgiveness,

00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:53.360
are not about pretending that your

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:55.120
problems are small. [music]

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:58.360
It is reminding

00:16:55.120 --> 00:17:01.640
that your life is [music] large.

00:16:58.360 --> 00:17:03.520
The best habits don't keep you from

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:04.431
getting lost.

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:04.640
They help you

00:17:04.431 --> 00:17:06.640
>> [music]

00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:07.880
>> find your way home.

00:17:06.640 --> 00:17:10.560
Thank you.

00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:10.560
And I love you.

